WEBVTT

NOTE Five-minute presenter draft, NOT synchronized to walkthrough.webm.
Record narration and retime these captions against the final video before sharing.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Project Krypto: governed AI decisioning.
Synthetic ETH/USDC engineering demo, not investment advice.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:45.000
A recommendation is not permission to act.
The model proposes; deterministic software authorizes.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:10.000
This isolated console uses mock providers and shadow mode.
No Azure account, customer data, or exchange orders are involved.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Inspect the account, rollout mode, and operator controls.
Charts are engineering fixtures, not this account's trading history.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:02:00.000
On a fresh account, the first run establishes a reference price.
That cold-start HOLD does not require model inference.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The next run receives a deterministic BUY proposal.
The static price has not dropped below its new reference.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Policy rejects the proposal. Inspect the journal and empty ledger.
Shadow records the outcome; it never executes a fill.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:30.000
These paused and locked states are browser-test fixtures.
They are not evidence that a market crash caused a lock.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Mandatory safety paths can bypass the model entirely.
Never clear a real lock or weaken authorization to improve a demo.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:15.000
The reusable pattern is structured advice, deterministic authorization,
run evidence, and explicit operator control.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Which customer workflow needs these boundaries?
Agree on synthetic inputs, forbidden actions, and measurable success.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Choose one workflow. Nominate a process owner and platform engineer
for a discovery workshop and a non-production pilot.
